<!-- ============================================================
     Microsoft SharePoint Server Deserialization of Untrusted Data RCE
     CVE-2026-58644 (CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data)
     Affected: SharePoint Server Subscription Edition, 2019,
               Enterprise Server 2016, SharePoint Online (on-prem)
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1190     - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell (TA0002 Execution)
       T1059.003 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Windows Command Shell
       T1105     - Ingress Tool Transfer (TA0011 C2)
       T1505.003 - Server Software Component: Web Shell (TA0003 Persistence)
       T1218     - System Binary Proxy Execution (TA0005 Defense Evasion)
       T1046     - Network Service Discovery (TA0007 Discovery)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-16, Federal Deadline 2026-07-19
     Rule IDs: 103979-103985
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-16

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-58644 is a critical-severity (CVSS critical) deserialization of
     untrusted data vulnerability in Microsoft SharePoint Server. Unlike
     CVE-2026-45659 (which required Site Member credentials), CVE-2026-58644
     is exploitable by an UNAUTHORIZED (unauthenticated) attacker with
     network access to the SharePoint HTTP endpoint. No credentials are
     required.

     The vulnerability exists in SharePoint's server-side processing of
     user-supplied data that is passed to .NET deserialization routines.
     When a specially crafted HTTP POST request is sent to specific
     SharePoint endpoints accessible without authentication, the server
     deserializes attacker-controlled .NET objects via BinaryFormatter or
     equivalent .NET serialization internals, achieving remote code
     execution in the context of the IIS worker process (w3wp.exe) running
     the SharePoint application pool.

     Pre-authentication distinguishes this CVE from prior SharePoint
     deserialization bugs and dramatically lowers the attack barrier:
     any network-reachable attacker can attempt exploitation without
     obtaining valid SharePoint credentials.

     Attack Chain:

       1. Reconnaissance: Attacker fingerprints SharePoint version via
          /_vti_bin/sitedata.asmx, /_api/contextinfo, or /_layouts/ responses.
          Version-specific gadget chains are selected accordingly.
       2. Payload Construction: Attacker generates a .NET deserialization
          payload using tooling (ysoserial.net, NDESERIALIZER) targeting
          gadget chains present in SharePoint's .NET assembly load path
          (e.g., ObjectDataProvider, TypeConfuseDelegate, ActivitySurrogateSelector).
          OOB callback gadgets (DNS or HTTP) are injected first to confirm
          code execution before staging a full implant.
       3. Delivery: Attacker sends an HTTP POST to a pre-auth accessible
          SharePoint endpoint containing the serialized payload. The POST
          body contains BinaryFormatter-serialized data (magic bytes AAEAAAD
          in base64) or SOAP-wrapped serialized objects.
       4. Execution: SharePoint deserializes the payload; the IIS worker
          process (w3wp.exe) executes the embedded gadget chain. The first
          observable signal is typically an OOB DNS lookup or HTTP callback
          from the server confirming RCE.
       5. Post-Exploitation: Common second-stage actions observed in the wild:
          - PowerShell download cradle to stage a reverse shell or Cobalt
            Strike beacon (w3wp.exe spawning powershell.exe)
          - Webshell drop to /_layouts/, /sites/[name]/, or %SystemDrive%\inetpub
            (w3wp.exe creating .aspx, .asmx, or .ashx files)
          - LOLBin execution for lateral movement staging (certutil, bitsadmin)
          - Host enumeration (whoami, ipconfig, nltest) for situational awareness
          - DNS OOB callback (nslookup / ping to attacker-controlled domain)
            to confirm RCE and exfiltrate preliminary host data

     Detection Layers:

     Layer 1 - Process Execution (Sysmon EventID 1, Wazuh SID 61603):
       Rule 103979 (level 0): Silent base anchor - w3wp.exe spawning any
         child process. All Layer 1 child rules depend on this anchor.
         Level 0 means no alert is generated for the anchor itself; it
         only serves as a correlation parent for the severity-bearing
         child rules.
       Rule 103980 (level 14): w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell, pwsh, or
         .NET compiler (csc.exe, vbc.exe). These are the highest-confidence
         process-layer signals. Deserialization gadget chains routinely
         invoke PowerShell as the first post-RCE action for download cradles
         or C# compilation of staged implants. Near-certain true positive.
       Rule 103981 (level 13): w3wp.exe spawning download/staging LOLBins
         (certutil, bitsadmin, mshta, wscript, cscript, curl, wget).
         These are used to retrieve second-stage payloads from attacker
         infrastructure immediately after RCE. High-confidence signal.
       Rule 103982 (level 12): w3wp.exe spawning host-enumeration or OOB
         callback utilities (whoami, hostname, ipconfig, nslookup, ping,
         nltest, net, arp). Covers both the standard post-exploit recon
         phase AND the out-of-band callback pattern: deserialization gadgets
         using DNS OOB to confirm execution call nslookup or ping with an
         attacker-controlled domain as the target, producing a detectable
         DNS query from the server.

     Layer 2 - File Creation (Sysmon EventID 11):
       Rule 103983 (level 15): w3wp.exe creating an ASPX, ASMX, ASHX, or
         SVC file. Under normal SharePoint operation the IIS worker process
         does not create new web script files. Any such file creation is a
         near-certain webshell drop indicating persistent backdoor access
         was established after the deserialization exploit landed. Requires
         immediate incident response.

     Layer 3 - HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs):
       Rule 103984 (level 9): POST to pre-authentication-accessible SharePoint
         endpoints. These endpoints do not require valid credentials and are
         the expected delivery vectors for CVE-2026-58644. Acts as an anchor
         for the HTTP-layer child rule.
       Rule 103985 (level 11): Child of 103984 - POST to SharePoint pre-auth
         endpoint returned HTTP 5xx. A deserialization exception before gadget
         chain completion surfaces as a 500 response. Active exploit probing
         or misconfigured payload delivery in progress.

     Log Source Prerequisites:
       Process/File layer (rules 103979-103983): Sysmon installed on Windows
         hosts running IIS/SharePoint with EventID 1 (Process Creation) and
         EventID 11 (File Created) enabled. Wazuh Sysmon integration assigns
         SID 61603 for Sysmon EventID 1. The file-creation rule (103983) uses
         decoded_as=json with win.eventdata.eventID field matching to
         distinguish EventID 11 from other Sysmon events.
       HTTP layer (rules 103984-103985): Traefik access logs in JSON format
         forwarded to Wazuh. Field names follow Traefik v2+ JSON log defaults:
         RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost, DownstreamStatus, ClientHost.
         Rules use decoded_as=json. No authentication header inspection is
         performed at the Wazuh layer; the anchor fires on all POSTs to
         the listed paths regardless of whether Authorization headers are present.

     Severity Tiers:
        0 - Process anchor (silent, correlation parent only)
       14 - w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell or .NET compiler (near-certain RCE)
       13 - w3wp.exe spawning download/staging LOLBins (payload delivery)
       12 - w3wp.exe spawning OOB/recon tools (DNS OOB callback or enumeration)
       15 - w3wp.exe creating web script file (webshell drop, critical)
        9 - HTTP POST to pre-auth SharePoint endpoint (anchor)
       11 - HTTP POST to pre-auth endpoint returning 5xx (exception probe)

     False Positive Guidance:
       103979: w3wp.exe legitimately spawns child processes during app pool
         recycling and IIS worker maintenance. However, the specific child
         process types in rules 103980-103982 have essentially no benign
         explanation in a SharePoint AppPool context.
       103980: PowerShell invocations from w3wp.exe have no legitimate
         operational justification in standard SharePoint deployments.
         SharePoint uses .NET managed code internally, not PowerShell.
         Custom SharePoint apps using PowerShell cmdlets should be inventoried;
         suppress for known-good application pool identities if required.
       103981: download LOLBins from w3wp.exe are unexpected in production.
         Staging environments running automated deployment tools may trigger
         this; baseline the source host and user context.
       103982: nslookup/ping from w3wp.exe may occur in custom SharePoint
         apps that perform DNS lookups for internal services. Correlate with
         destination domain: known-internal destinations are likely benign,
         external or randomly-named destinations indicate OOB DNS callbacks.
       103983: Near-zero false positive rate. w3wp.exe writing web script
         files is not a routine SharePoint operation in production environments.
         Any match should trigger immediate incident response.
       103984: Legitimate SharePoint REST API clients, workflow engines, and
         search crawlers issue POST requests to these paths. Alert volume will
         be high; use as enrichment anchor. Escalate based on child rules
         (103985) or correlation with process-layer events (103980-103983).
       103985: SharePoint returns 500 for misconfigured requests, expired
         tokens, and transient server errors. Meaningful signal when the same
         source IP generates repeated 500 responses across multiple SharePoint
         path patterns in a short window, or when correlated with host-side
         process events.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,sharepoint,cisa_kev,cve_2026_58644,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: Process Execution (Sysmon EventID 1 - Wazuh SID 61603)
       Rule IDs: 103979-103982

       CVE-2026-58644 achieves code execution within the SharePoint IIS
       application pool (w3wp.exe). All post-RCE actions visible at the
       process-execution layer originate as w3wp.exe children. The silent
       anchor (103979) correlates parent-child process relationships; the
       severity-bearing child rules (103980-103982) fire on specific
       child process types that have no legitimate operational justification
       in a production SharePoint deployment.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: w3wp.exe spawning any child process (silent, level 0).

       SharePoint runs in IIS worker processes (w3wp.exe). Under normal
       operation the AppPool executes .NET managed code internally without
       spawning external child processes. Any child process spawned by
       w3wp.exe is anomalous and warrants inspection. This silent anchor
       at level 0 generates no alert itself; it is the if_sid parent for
       rules 103980-103982, enabling severity escalation based on the
       specific child process type.

       SID 61603 is the Wazuh built-in rule for Sysmon EventID 1
       (Process Creation). win.eventdata.parentImage is the full image
       path of the parent process from the Sysmon CreateProcess event. -->
  <rule id="103979" level="0">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w3wp\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: w3wp.exe spawned child process $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) (base anchor for deserialization RCE detection)</description>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,iis_worker,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- w3wp.exe spawning PowerShell, pwsh, or .NET compiler.

       PowerShell (powershell.exe, pwsh.exe) and the Roslyn C# compiler
       (csc.exe, vbc.exe) are the primary first-stage post-exploitation
       tools invoked by .NET deserialization gadget chains targeting
       SharePoint. Typical patterns observed in the wild:

         - powershell.exe -enc [base64]   : download cradle for reverse shell
         - powershell.exe -nop -w hidden  : Cobalt Strike stager
         - csc.exe /out:[tmp].dll         : in-memory C# compilation of implant

       There is no legitimate operational reason for the SharePoint IIS
       worker process to spawn PowerShell or a .NET compiler. Any match
       against this rule should be treated as a confirmed deserialization
       RCE and escalated immediately. -->
  <rule id="103980" level="14">
    <if_sid>103979</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh|csc|vbc|jsc)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: w3wp.exe spawned $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user)  -  near-certain deserialization RCE; PowerShell download cradle or .NET in-memory compilation post-exploit; initiate incident response (T1190/T1059.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,rce,powershell,high_confidence,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- w3wp.exe spawning download or staging LOLBins.

       After achieving RCE via deserialization, threat actors retrieve
       second-stage payloads from attacker-controlled infrastructure using
       native Windows binaries (Living Off The Land Binaries):

         - certutil.exe -urlcache -f [url]    : HTTP download, decodes base64
         - bitsadmin /transfer [url] [dest]   : BITS download, evades some AV
         - mshta.exe http://...               : remote HTA payload execution
         - wscript.exe / cscript.exe          : script execution
         - curl.exe / wget.exe                : direct HTTP download

       All of these are standard Microsoft-provided or OS-bundled binaries.
       None have a legitimate use case as direct children of the SharePoint
       IIS worker process in production. A match here indicates an attacker
       is staging additional capabilities after initial RCE. -->
  <rule id="103981" level="13">
    <if_sid>103979</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(certutil|bitsadmin|mshta|wscript|cscript|curl|wget)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: w3wp.exe spawned download LOLBin $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user)  -  second-stage payload staging after deserialization RCE; attacker retrieving implant from external infrastructure (T1190/T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,rce,lolbin,payload_staging,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- w3wp.exe spawning host-enumeration or OOB callback utilities.

       This rule covers two distinct but overlapping detection patterns:

       1. Out-of-band (OOB) DNS/ICMP callback confirmation:
          ysoserial.net and similar .NET deserialization tools support
          "DNS OOB" gadget chains that call nslookup.exe or ping.exe
          with an attacker-controlled domain as the argument. The resulting
          DNS query to an external resolver confirms code execution to the
          attacker without producing a visible HTTP response. This is the
          most common technique used to validate deserialization RCE before
          deploying a full implant.

          Examples:
            nslookup.exe [attacker-owned-domain]
            ping.exe -n 1 [attacker-owned-domaingt 2. Post-exploit host enumeration (first-stage recon):
          After confirming RCE via OOB callback, attackers issue rapid
          host-enumeration commands to gather situational awareness before
          lateral movement:

            whoami.exe           : current process identity
            hostname.exe         : system hostname
            ipconfig.exe /all    : network configuration
            nltest.exe /domain_trusts : AD domain trust enumeration
            net.exe / net1.exe   : local users, groups, shares, sessions
            arp.exe -a           : local ARP table for adjacent host discovery

       Correlate the child process command-line arguments with external
       threat intel to distinguish OOB callback (external/random domain
       targets in nslookup/ping) from internal reconnaissance. -->
  <rule id="103982" level="12">
    <if_sid>103979</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(nslookup|ping|hostname|whoami|ipconfig|nltest|net|net1|arp|tracert|systeminfo|tasklist)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: w3wp.exe spawned $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) as $(win.eventdata.user)  -  OOB callback or post-exploit host enumeration; DNS OOB (nslookup/ping to attacker domain) confirms deserialization RCE; review child process command line for external domain targets (T1190/T1046)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1046</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,rce,oob_callback,enumeration,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: File Creation (Sysmon EventID 11)
       Rule ID: 103983

       Web script file creation by the IIS worker process is the clearest
       indicator that a persistent webshell has been deployed after the
       initial deserialization exploit. Unlike process-execution events
       (which may have short-lived child processes), a webshell file persists
       on disk and provides the attacker with durable remote access.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- w3wp.exe creating a web script file (webshell drop, critical).

       Sysmon EventID 11 (File Created) captures the image path of the
       process that created the file. Under normal SharePoint operation
       w3wp.exe does not create new ASPX, ASMX, ASHX, or SVC files.
       These file types are the standard web script formats used to host
       webshells on ASP.NET / IIS platforms.

       Common webshell deployment paths observed in SharePoint exploitation:
         - C:\inetpub\wwwroot\wss\VirtualDirectories\...\[name].aspx
         - C:\Program Files\Common Files\Microsoft Shared\web server extensions\
           \LAYOUTS\[name].aspx
         - C:\Program Files\Common Files\Microsoft Shared\web server extensions\
           \isapi\[name].asmx

       Any file creation event matching this rule requires immediate incident
       response: identify the created file, analyze for webshell content,
       remove it, audit SharePoint access logs for subsequent use, and initiate
       IR for full scope assessment.

       win.eventdata.eventID is matched as pcre2 field (not decoded_as event
       number) because some Wazuh Sysmon decoder variants include it as a
       string field in the decoded JSON. The decoded_as=json ensures the
       Wazuh JSON decoder processes the log before field extraction. -->
  <rule id="103983" level="15">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="win.eventdata.eventID" type="pcre2">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w3wp\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\.(aspx|asmx|ashx|svc|cshtml|vbhtml)$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644 CRITICAL: w3wp.exe created web script file $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  WEBSHELL DROP confirmed; persistent backdoor established after deserialization RCE; initiate immediate incident response, isolate host, preserve forensic image (T1505.003/T1190)</description>
    <options>alert_by_email</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1505.003</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,webshell,persistence,critical,high_confidence,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 3: HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs)
       Rule IDs: 103984-103985

       CVE-2026-58644 is triggered by an HTTP POST to SharePoint endpoints
       accessible without authentication. Traefik access logs in JSON format
       provide the HTTP-layer visibility needed to detect the exploit delivery
       phase before any process events fire on the SharePoint host.

       Pre-authentication endpoints targeted by this CVE include:
         /_vti_bin/    - Legacy SharePoint SOAP WCF services (client.svc,
                         authentication.asmx, webs.asmx). These expose
                         .NET serialization surfaces pre-authentication.
         /_layouts/    - SharePoint layout pages. Some layouts handle
                         ViewState or form body deserialization without
                         requiring authentication.
         /_api/contextinfo - REST endpoint that returns the form digest
                         value; commonly probed to fingerprint SharePoint
                         version before exploit payload delivery.
         /_api/web     - SharePoint REST API root; some actions pre-auth.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- POST to pre-authentication-accessible SharePoint endpoint (anchor).

       Fires on any HTTP POST to the SharePoint endpoint patterns listed
       above. This is a broad anchor; by itself it generates level 9
       informational alerts that are useful for correlation. The majority
       of these requests are benign (SharePoint REST clients, search crawlers,
       workflow engines). Alert volume will be high on active SharePoint
       deployments. Use this rule primarily as a correlation anchor:
       escalate when it fires in combination with process-layer events
       (103980-103983) on the same destination host within a short window,
       or when the child rule 103985 fires for the same source IP.

       RequestPath is matched with pcre2 against a set of path prefixes
       known to be pre-auth accessible and relevant to deserialization
       attack delivery. Traefik field names follow the v2+ JSON access
       log format. -->
  <rule id="103984" level="9">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/_vti_bin/(?:client\.svc|authentication\.asmx|webs\.asmx|sitedata\.asmx|usergroup\.asmx|lists\.asmx|search\.asmx)|/_layouts/(?:15|16)/[^/]+\.(?:aspx|asmx|ashx)|/_api/(?:contextinfo|web(?:$|/))|/sites/[^/]+/_vti_bin/|/sites/[^/]+/_layouts/)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: HTTP POST to pre-auth-accessible SharePoint endpoint from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath)  -  potential unauthenticated deserialization exploit delivery (T1190); correlate with host-side process events on the SharePoint server</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,http_probe,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to SharePoint pre-auth endpoint returning HTTP 5xx.

       A .NET deserialization exception produces an HTTP 500 Internal Server
       Error before the gadget chain completes successfully. Attackers
       probing CVE-2026-58644 with iterative payload variants (different
       gadget chains, different BinaryFormatter encoding, different target
       types) generate a sequence of 500 responses as they refine the
       exploit. A single 500 may be a transient SharePoint error; repeated
       500 responses from the same source IP against pre-auth endpoint
       patterns is a strong exploitation signal.

       HTTP 5xx (500-599) is matched broadly: some deserialization exceptions
       surface as 502 (if Traefik receives no response from the upstream)
       or 503 (if the AppPool crashes and IIS returns service unavailable).
       A 500 on _vti_bin or _layouts where the post body caused the exception
       is the canonical exploit-probe response. -->
  <rule id="103985" level="11">
    <if_sid>103984</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^5\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: POST to pre-auth SharePoint endpoint at $(RequestHost)$(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus)  -  deserialization exception likely; active exploit probe or gadget chain refinement in progress; check for repeated 500s from same source IP and correlate with host-side w3wp.exe events (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,deserialization_probe,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
